    "description": "Synthesizers\nAccessories\nSoftware Store\nThe Wave\nSupport\nSynthesizers\nAccessories\nSoftware Store\nThe Wave\nSupport\nFeatured Products\nMessenger\nSYNTHESIZERS\n\nA New Method Of Sonic Experimentation, Communication, And Connection Has Arrived\n\nDISCOVER\n\nLabyrinth\nSYNTHESIZERS\n\nHow Deep Does The Rabbit Hole Go? Meet Labyrinth, An Idea Machine That Aims To Answer That Very Question.\n\nDISCOVER\n\nGet The Latest Moog News\n\nThis site is protected by reCAPTCHA and the Google\nPrivacy Policy and Terms of Service apply.\n\nHome\n\n/\n\nSynthesizers\n\n/\n\nMuse\nMuse\n\nFind a Dealer\n\nThe Ultimate Live Performance Synth\n\nMeet Muse, an eight-voice analog bi-timbral polyphonic synthesizer — designed for limitless musical exploration and sonic discovery.\n\nDownloads\n\n\nDownload Firmware v1.4.0\n\nIntroduction\n\nOverview\n\nVideo\n\nSpecs\n\nResources\n\nWalkthroughs\n\n1\n2\n3\n4\n\nMeet Muse\n\nMuse is an intuitive and powerful instrument built on the rich history of Moog that simultaneously gazes boldly into the future. This studio or performance centerpiece is driven by eight analog voices, each with two VCOs, modulation VCO, dual VCFs, and a stereo VCA — all with that recognizable, kinetic analog quality.\n \nMuse represents a melting pot of classic Moog designs paired with forward-thinking controls in one complete instrument. We’ve selected the very best of vintage Moog circuits to create Muse — inspired by Moog Voyager oscillators, MF-102 Ring Modulator, CP3 Mixer, 902 VCA module, and a 904A Filter.\n \nWith 16 banks of 16 patches, you’ll encounter lush pads, organic FM plucks, attention-grabbing leads, subterranean basses, stage-oriented key splits, as well as two banks reserved for your own sonic discoveries. Explore and express yourself endlessly with cutting-edge digital controls for powerful modulation capabilities, dedicated macro control, customizable envelopes, and multiple flexible LFOs.\n\nMuse was created for, and by, modern musicians, producers, and sound designers who appreciate performance-oriented controls, intuitive knob-per-function layout, and “More” menus for every module. Built for stage and studio with a 61-key Fatar keyboard with velocity and channel aftertouch, a left-hand controller with pitch and mod wheels, and a programmable Macro knob makes the experience of interacting with Muse inspiring, intriguing, and innately playable.\n\nThe instrument’s hypnotic stereo Diffusion Delay™ processor was inspired by golden-era vintage digital rack delays with a powerful set of diffusion, multi-tap, and filter behaviors that add dimension, complexity, and depth to any patch. Muse’s 1.4.0 firmware update further expands the Diffusion Delay’s modulation parameters by introducing six new global modulation sources. This opens up a whole new world of sonic possibilities in Muse from pitch-shifting choruses and randomly modulation-diffuse spaces to dynamic delay sends or aftertouch feedback control.\n\nIn addition to Muse’s creative immediacy, the powerful arpeggiator invites you to generate melodic ideas with compelling patterns and rhythms. With Muse’s deep sequencer, you'll unravel unique patterns and gestures — further develop these discoveries with parameter recording, generative/probabilistic functions, and detailed editing of every note in the sequence.\n\nMoog Muse | Factory Preset Tour | All Sounds, No Talking\n\nJoin Lisa Bella Donna and Max Ravitz on an hour-long tour through the factory library of patches for Muse – from searing leads and thumping basses through delicate textural pads and everything in between. All sounds recorded direct from Muse with no external effects added.\n\nClassic Moog Sound, Modernized\n\nA melting pot of classic Moog analog circuit designs is paired with forward-thinking digital controls in one complete instrument. Muse combines discrete oscillators derived from the Minimoog Voyager, brought together by a saturating mixer, dual transistor ladder filters, and stereo discrete VCAs all inspired by Moog modular circuits.\n\nReady for the Stage or Studio\n\nWith performance-oriented controls, an intuitive knob-per-function layout, and “More” menus for every module, Muse was created for, and by, modern musicians, producers, and sound designers. In addition to Muse’s creative immediacy, a powerful arpeggiator, chord memory, and a polyphonic sequencer provide deep editing capabilities and generative behaviors that fuel musical expeditions.\n\nFeatures That Inspire\n\nA hypnotic stereo Diffusion Delay™ processor was inspired by golden-era vintage digital rack delays with a unique and powerful set of diffusion, multi-tap, and filter behaviors. Craft complex echoes and diffused tonal smears that add dimension and depth to any patch. Take your modulation exploration a step further with the Diffusion Delay’s expanded modulation options introduced in the latest Muse firmware update.\n\nReflections with Lisa Bella Donna\n\nSynthesist, composer, and sound designer Lisa Bella Donna explores the sonic possibilities of the Moog Muse eight voice polyphonic analog synthesizer. Featuring a metallic loop of Muse-made bell tones alongside luscious chords and soaring leads, Lisa’s emotive and immersive performance resonates within the walls of the historic Sowden House in Los Angeles, California.  \n\nLearn more about Lisa Bella Donna. \n\nAll sounds from the Moog Muse \n\nReflections with Misha Mansoor\n\nMulti-instrumentalist and composer Misha Mansoor explores the sonic possibilities of the Moog Muse eight voice polyphonic analog synthesizer. Focused on an adapted version of the Muse Runner patch, Misha explores the aftertouch swells and striking sonic depths of Muse in this warm and haunting performance from the historic Sowden House in Los Angeles, California.\n\nLearn more about Misha Mansoor.\n\nAll sounds from the Moog Muse \n\nReflections with Stephan Moccio\n\nComposer and producer Stephan Moccio explores the sonic possibilities of the Moog Muse eight-voice polyphonic analog synthesizer. Playing in a neo-renaissance style, Stephan explores the saturated staccato timbres and tight yet diffuse delay textures of Muse in this bright and punchy performance from the historic Sowden House in Los Angeles, California.\n\nLearn more about Stephan Moccio.\n\nAll sounds from the Moog Muse\n\nMuse Specifications\n\nSYNTHESIZER TYPE\nPolyphonic, Bi-timbral Analog Synthesizer\nSOUND ENGINE\nAnalog\nPOLYPHONY\n8 Voices\nKEYBED\n61 full-size semi-weighted Fatar keybed with Velocity and channel Aftertouch\nCONTROLLERS\nPitch Wheel, Modulation Wheel, Macro Knob, Keyboard Octave switch, Hold switch, Sustain Pedal input, Expression Pedal input – all pedal functions are assignable\nPANEL CONTROLS\n44 knobs, 16 sliders, 129 buttons – OLED screen\nANALOG VOLTAGE-CONTROLLED OSCILLATORS\n(x2) Selectable Triangle/Sawtooth mix, variable width Pulse wave, Octave (16’, 8’, 4’, 2’), Frequency (+/- 7 Semitones), Wave Mix (blends Triangle/Sawtooth with variable Pulse wave), FM routing and amount, Hard sync\nSHOW FULL LIST +\n\nRear Panel\n\nAUDIO OUTPUTS\nMain Left, Main Right (¼\" TRS)\nHEADPHONES\nStereo ¼\" (located on the front edge of the Left Hand Controller)\nPEDAL INPUTS\nSustain, Expression (¼\" TRS; Configurable through Mod Map or for use as ¼\" TS CV inputs)\nKEYBED\n61 full-size semi-weighted Fatar keybed with Velocity and channel Aftertouch\nCONTROL VOLTAGE INPUTS\nCV IN 1, CV IN 2 (1/8” TS)\nCONTROL VOLTAGE OUTPUTS\nCV OUT 1, CV OUT 2 (1/8” TS)\nANALOG CLOCK INPUT\nCLOCK IN (1/8” TS)\nANALOG CLOCK OUTPUT\nCLOCK OUT (1/8” TS)\nSHOW FULL LIST +\n\nExplore Muse\n\nSynthesis is a journey, and we hope that these resources help guide your explorations with Muse. On this page, you will find a growing collection of downloads, tutorial videos, tips, and other helpful resources. Each artist's relationship with synthesis is unique, and we hope that these guides can help inspire you to discover your own path.\n\nDownload the Muse Manual\n\nDownload the Muse Quickstart Guide\n\nDownload Firmware v1.4.0\n\nDownload Patch Libraries\n\nFrequently Asked Questions\n\nVideo Walkthroughs\n\nQuick Assign\n\nIn this video we will look at the quick ASSIGN buttons found on the front panel of Muse and explore how to use them to make quick modulation assignments.\n\nWe start with an initialized patch to quickly build up an interesting sound from scratch. The ASSIGNABLE CONTROLLERS module contains assignment buttons for common modulation sources such as the keyboard, mod wheel, or aftertouch, and we use that section to assign aftertouch to control vibrato depth via the PITCH LFO. We then take a look at our modulation routing in the MOD MAP, edit the depth of modulation (holding SHIFT for fine tuning), and add some slew via the transfer functions available in the MOD MAP. Each TIMBRE in a Muse PATCH can have 16 modulation slots and quick assign can help you construct deep and evocative sounds very quickly.\n\nQuick Assign\n\nIn this video we will look at the quick ASSIGN buttons found on the front panel of Muse and explore how to use them to make quick modulation assignments.\n\nMacro Controls\n\nIn this video we explore the various macro controls on Muse and how you can use them for patch morphing and modulation.\n\nChord Mode\n\nIn this video we look at the CHORD module of Muse and how to use it for both traditional and atypical chord memory functions.\n\nDiffusion Delay\n\nIn this video we take a look at the unique DIFFUSION DELAY effect on Muse and how to use it to add space and movement to Muse’s analog voices.\n\nSequencer Basics\n\nIn this video we dip our toes into the SEQUENCER of Muse and start building sequences from scratch.\n\nArpeggiator Basics\n\nIn this video we explore the ARPEGGIATOR section of Muse and how to use its unique programmable functions to create dynamic and mesmerizing arpeggiated patterns.\n\nGesture Mode\n\nIn this video, we explore Muse's Gesture Mode within the step sequencer!\n\nPreset Management\n\nIn this video, we walk you through the bank and preset management systems in Moog Muse!\n\nMod Oscillator\n\nIn this video, we explore the Modulation Oscillator of Moog Muse.\n\nDual Filter Configuration\n\nIn this video, we explore the configuration of the Dual Filters of Moog Muse.\n\nPer-Timbre Sequencer Probability\n\nIn this video, we explore how to use the new per-timbre probability options added to Muse's sequencer in the new v1.4 firmware.\n\nDynamic Chorus\n\nIn this video, we explore how to use some of the new features in Muse firmware version v1.4 to create dynamic chorus and interactive diffusion patches.\n\nFading Modulation\n\nIn this video, we explore how to use some of the new features in Muse firmware version v1.4 to create modulation routings that fade over the course of a note’s duration.\n\nLFO Envelope Mode\n\nIn this video, we explore how to use the envelope mode added to Muse's LFOs in the v1.4 firmware.\n\nRelated Products\n\nSpectravox\nSYNTHESIZERS\nSpectravox, a semi-modular analog spectral processor, is a groundbreaking tool for sculpting sound.\n\nDISCOVER\n\nLabyrinth\nSYNTHESIZERS\nHow deep does the rabbit hole go? Meet Labyrinth, an idea machine that aims to answer that very question.\n\nDISCOVER\n\nSynthesizers\n\nDISCOVER\n\nAccessories\n\nDISCOVER\n\nSoftware Store\n\nSHOP SOFTWARE\n\nSupport\nForum\nHelpdesk\nKnowledge base\nDownloads\nAll Product Downloads\nPresets\nUser Manuals\nPatches\nFirmware Updates\nThe Wave\nView all articles\nRSS Feed\nAbout Us\nCareers\nTerms Of Use\nPrivacy\nFind A Dealer\nGet The Latest Moog News\n\nThis site is protected by reCAPTCHA and the Google\nPrivacy Policy and Terms of Service apply.",
